BT /F2 14 Tf 56 774 Td (Chapter 5: The Attention Mechanism) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 747 Td (Attention is the operation that lets a model decide, for every token, which other) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 732 Td (tokens matter. Each token is projected into three vectors: a query, a key and a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 717 Td (value. The relevance of token j to token i is the dot product between the query of i) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 702 Td (and the key of j. Those scores are divided by the square root of the head dimension) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 687 Td (to keep them numerically stable, passed through a softmax so they sum to one, and) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 672 Td (then used as weights to average the value vectors. This is called scaled dot product) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 657 Td (attention.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 634 Td (Self attention means queries, keys and values all come from the same sequence, so) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 619 Td (the sequence attends to itself. Cross attention means the queries come from one) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 604 Td (sequence and the keys and values from another, which is how a decoder consults an) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 589 Td (encoder. Multi head attention runs several attention operations in parallel with) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 574 Td (different projections, so one head can track syntax while another tracks long range) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 559 Td (topic information, and the results are concatenated and projected back.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 536 Td (In a decoder, attention is causally masked: a token may only attend to itself and to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 521 Td (tokens before it, otherwise the model could cheat by looking at the answer it is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 506 Td (being asked to predict. The cost of attention grows with the square of the sequence) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 491 Td (length, which is the main reason context windows are limited and the main motivation) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 476 Td (for efficient attention variants such as FlashAttention.) Tj ET
BT /F2 14 Tf 56 445 Td (Chapter 6: Embeddings) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 418 Td (An embedding is a dense vector of floating point numbers that represents a piece of) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 403 Td (content in a way a machine can compare. A sentence embedding model maps a whole) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 388 Td (sentence or paragraph to a single vector, typically with 384, 768 or 1536) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 373 Td (dimensions, so that texts with similar meaning land close together in the vector) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 358 Td (space even when they share no vocabulary.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 335 Td (Closeness is normally measured with cosine similarity, the cosine of the angle) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 320 Td (between two vectors. It ranges from minus one for opposite meaning to one for) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 305 Td (identical direction, and it ignores vector length, which makes it robust to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 290 Td (differences in text length. When vectors are normalised to unit length, cosine) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 275 Td (similarity and the dot product are the same computation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 252 Td (Embeddings are what make semantic search possible. A keyword index can only find) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 237 Td (documents that literally contain the words of the query, so a question about a car) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 222 Td (will not match a document about an automobile. An embedding index compares meaning,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 56 207 Td (so the two match even with no shared token.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9 Tf 56 40 Td (Page 3 of 4) Tj ET
endstream
